hi I'm Emily Tunnel a research associate  with PETA thanks for stopping by my  poster when I was in graduate school for  neuroscience the forced swim test is  something that would come up in  discussions about as an example of an  animal behavioral test that was often  used to draw incorrect or at best  specious conclusions about an animal's  mood if you're unfamiliar with the for  swim test it was popularized by Roger /  salt it's also called the pour salt swim  test in the late 70s so in this test a  small animal typically a mouse or rat  also sometimes a gerbil hamster or  guinea pig it's placed into a cylinder  that's been filled with water  of course the animal will swim around  furiously try to climb up the sides of  the tank dive to the bottom to try to  find an escape but eventually they will  stop swimming and just start floating  her salt notice that when an animal was  acutely dosed with a tricyclic  antidepressant compounds which were used  at the time and are still used today  that they would struggle for longer  before stopping to float so this lit led  him to interpret floating as a sign of  depression or what he called behavioral  despair since acute administration of an  antidepressant compound would reduce the  amount of time the animals spent  floating since that time that that  interpretation has stuck with a lot of  people and the for swim test has made an  appearance in thousands of publications  and in an FDA investigation on new drug  applications for antidepressant  compounds also since that time a lot of  people have argued that floating is not  a sign of behavioral despair at all but  it's actually a positive trait for  survival animals that stopped swimming  and float for longer are saving their  energy they're adapting to their  situation others have noted really the  overall concept that this very simple  behavioral readout can in no way stand  for the chronic subjective emotional  state of human depression  so due to this controversy we wanted to  know how often pharmaceutical companies  these are entities whose bottom line  depends on turning out successful  therapeutics have used the for swim test  and if their use of it had resulted in  the successful marketing of  antidepressant drugs so we chose four  very well-known pharmaceutical companies  Abbott Labs which is a V which is  formerly Abbott Labs bristol-myers  Squibb Eli Lilly and Pfizer we looked on  PubMed and Google Scholar to locate any  published papers or patent applications  where they presented forced swim test  data we then used we went through those  publications and identified each  compound that was used in for swim tests  we then used these same databases as  well as clinical trials.gov  to follow these compounds through their  development to see had they gone on to  be tested in human depression what's  what sort of happened so in our research  with these four companies we identified  forty seven compounds that were given to  almost 5,000 animals that were used in  for swim tests by these four companies  only 14 of these compounds which you'll  see in the gray table on my poster 14 of  these compounds that reduced immobility  in the fourth swim test which means by  that conclusion they should have some  efficacy for human depression 14 of  these 14 have also been tested in human  depression so then we asked you know  what what did these human tests show did  the four swim test was the four swim  tests correct and predicting that they  were efficacious and are these compounds  currently used as successful human  antidepressants the result was that no  none of the 14 compounds are currently  marketed as as antidepressants and used  by humans only three of them showed to  be efficacious for human depression  three of them are still undergoing  clinical trials  and the remaining 11 compounds we found  that the foursome test was not  predictive of how of the efficacy of  these drugs for human depression so  that's definite 11 out of the 14 I'm  sorry so that's 8 out of the 14 that  were absolutely nada fication 3 you're  still pending so that's at least a 57  percent chance of the force one test  giving a false conclusion that's higher  than chance I'm flipping a coin of the  three compounds that did reduce the  mobility in the porcelain test and were  efficacious for human depression they  are not currently marketed because of  other side effects that most of which  were not predicted by the preclinical  animal experiments so this is a very  small sample size for companies 12 out  of 47 compounds but we're doing more  work in this area and we and we hope to  publish this data soon but if further  data corroborate these findings that the  for swim test is less predictive than  chance at saying whether or not a  compound could be efficacious for human  depression then we argue that there  would be very little reason for  pharmaceutical companies to continue to  use the for swim test the reason for the  FDA to consider it as a viable test in  new drug applications or really little  reason that for it to be used in basic  behavioral research when the for swim  tests animals are subjected to the  terror of near drowning and many have  drowned in these tests due to  inattentive experimenters or in early  versions of the test when before I  Eclipse they didn't really have to  rescue the animals from the situation  but so it's it's cruel at its base but  it really misleads drug development and  in turn doesn't help suffering patients  so if you'd like to learn more about  this I'd love to talk to you about it  you can reach me at Emily T at PETA org  thanks so much
 

